@node D-Bus Interfaces
@section Interfaces and Object Hierarchy

@subsection Object hierarchy

Phase 1 ships exactly one object: @code{/org/cmacs/Editor}.
Everything else is layered on the same path as separate interfaces
(per the freedesktop convention) so the introspection XML composes
cleanly.

@example
/org/cmacs/Editor                         [Phase 1 root]
  +- org.freedesktop.DBus.Introspectable  (auto, GDBus)
  +- org.freedesktop.DBus.Peer            (auto, bus daemon)
  +- org.freedesktop.DBus.Properties      (lazy; populated by Phase 2)
  +- org.freedesktop.DBus.ObjectManager   (registered, empty children)
  +- org.cmacs.Editor1                    (back-compat surface)
  +- org.cmacs.Editor1.Events             (shipped: events firehose + named signals)

[Phase 2-5 will add:]
/org/cmacs/Editor/Buffer/<id>             per-buffer object
/org/cmacs/Editor/Frame/<n>               per-frame object
/org/cmacs/Editor/Window/<n>              per-window object
/org/cmacs/Editor/Process/<id>            per-subprocess object
/org/cmacs/Editor/SearchProvider          GNOME Shell SearchProvider2
/org/cmacs/Editor/Watch/<token>           per-watch handle
/org/cmacs/Editor/Request/<token>         long-running operation handle
/org/cmacs/Editor/MediaPlayer2            MPRIS-as-buffer-nav (opt-in)
@end example

@subsection org.freedesktop.DBus.Introspectable (auto)

Provided by GDBus for free on every registered object.  Returns the
introspection XML for whatever interfaces are currently registered.

@example
gdbus introspect --session \
    --dest org.cmacs.Editor \
    --object-path /org/cmacs/Editor
@end example

Output covers every interface registered on the path, merged.

@subsection org.freedesktop.DBus.Peer (auto)

Provided by the bus daemon for any connection.  Two trivial methods
that any consumer can use as a liveness probe.

@table @code
@item Ping ()
Reply with empty body if connection live
@item GetMachineId () -> s
Stable machine UUID per @file{/etc/machine-id}
@end table

Useful for health checks:

@example
if gdbus call --session --dest org.cmacs.Editor \
       --object-path /org/cmacs/Editor \
       --method org.freedesktop.DBus.Peer.Ping 2>/dev/null; then
    echo "cmacs is responsive"
fi
@end example

@subsection org.freedesktop.DBus.Properties

Standard freedesktop pattern: @code{Get(s,s) -> v},
@code{Set(s,s,v)}, @code{GetAll(s) -> a@{sv@}}, signal
@code{PropertiesChanged(s, a@{sv@}, as)}.

Phase 1 registers this iface lazily --- it appears on a path the
first time a module calls @code{cmacs_dbus_register_property} for
that path.  Phase 1 modules don't yet register any properties, so on
a fresh service @code{GetAll} over @code{org.cmacs.Editor1} returns
an empty dict.  The full surface lights up in Phase 2.

@subsection org.freedesktop.DBus.ObjectManager

Standard pattern for services with dynamic objects.

@table @code
@item GetManagedObjects () -> a@{oa@{sa@{sv@}@}@}
Snapshot of every (path, iface, properties) tuple
@item InterfacesAdded signal (o, a@{sa@{sv@}@})
Fires when a new (path, iface) appears
@item InterfacesRemoved signal (o, as)
Fires when (path, iface) disappears
@end table

In Phase 1 the registry is empty.  Phase 2 will populate it from
per-resource modules so the natural client pattern becomes:
enumerate once with @code{GetManagedObjects}, then watch the signals.

@subsection org.cmacs.Editor1 (back-compat)

This is the wire surface the cmacs D-Bus subsystem exposed before
the Phase 1 refactor.  Everything here is preserved bit-for-bit:
existing @command{cmacsgi} consumers, ad-hoc shell scripts, and any
third-party tool that calls @code{org.cmacs.Editor1.Eval} and
friends keeps working.

Six core methods plus 23 @code{Gowl*} methods (when
@option{--with-cmacs-gowl}); see @ref{D-Bus Methods} for the full
reference.

Phase 3 will introduce typed sub-interfaces
(@code{org.cmacs.Editor1.Buffer}, @code{.Search}, @code{.VC},
@code{.Pkg}, @code{.Monitor}, @code{.Bar}, @code{.Cintrospect},
@code{.Cpatch}, ...) that mirror the cmacsgi group structure with
proper return types.  The @code{Editor1} surface stays untouched.

@subsection Inspecting the live surface

@example
# What's actually exposed right now?
gdbus introspect --session \
    --dest org.cmacs.Editor \
    --object-path /org/cmacs/Editor \
    --xml
@end example

This dumps the raw introspection XML, the source of truth.  Pipe
through @command{xmllint --format -} for readability.  D-Spy
(@command{flatpak run org.gnome.dspy}) gives you the same thing as a
GUI tree.

@subsection Phase 6: MCP-parity interfaces

Every MCP tool capability now has a typed D-Bus method, so AI agents
(MCP) and scripts/humans (@pxref{emacsctl CLI}) see the same
surface.  One interface per subsystem, registered at
@file{/org/cmacs/Editor} and gated on the matching
@code{HAVE_CMACS_*} build flag: @code{Editor1.Crispy},
@code{.Bacon}, @code{.Eshell} (@file{cmacs-dbus-iface-lang.c});
@code{.Edit}; @code{.Input}; @code{.Debug}; @code{.Ai};
@code{.Gsurf}; @code{.GnusEye}; @code{.Podomation}; @code{.Video},
@code{.Audio}, @code{.Speech} (@file{cmacs-dbus-iface-media.c});
@code{.Lrg}; @code{.Instance} (identity: pid, version, features[]);
and @code{.Log} (@code{RecentMessages} plus the live
@code{MessageLogged} signal).

Earlier interfaces also grew parity methods: BufferManager
@code{Create/Switch/Save}, WindowManager
@code{Delete/SelectByBuffer}, ProcessManager @code{SendTo}, Project
@code{ListFiles/ReadFile/WriteFile}, and Compositor
@code{FocusClient/CloseClient/SetClientGeometry/SetLayout/
WorkspaceList/WorkspaceSwitch/Screenshot}.

Sync discipline: adding a tool in
@file{cmacs/mcp/cmacs-mcp-tools-*.c} requires a matching method in
the paired @file{cmacs/dbus/cmacs-dbus-iface-*.c} (and vice versa);
each file's header comment names its partner.
